# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------
# THREE connection urls on purpose — each has a distinct JOB, and they must not
# collapse into one another:
#
#   MIGRATE/DATABASE_URL  → OWNER. Runs migrations (`db:migrate` / `db:reset`).
#   APP_DATABASE_URL      → the NON-owner app role. The running app SERVES with it.
#   SQL_TEST_DATABASE_URL → the NON-owner app role. Every TEST connects with it.
#
# OWNER (superuser): runs migrations (`npm run db:migrate` / `npm run db:reset`).
# Whoever creates a table owns it, and a table's OWNER bypasses that table's own
# RLS policies — so the runner must connect as the owner, never as a test role.
#   MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL wins if set; DATABASE_URL is the fallback the runner
#   and the tests' `migrateUrl()` read.
MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres@127.0.0.1:55499/warehouse"
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres@127.0.0.1:55499/warehouse"

# APP (non-owner `authenticated`): the role the RUNNING APP serves as. It must
# NOT be the owner: a table's owner (or any superuser / `BYPASSRLS` role) bypasses
# that table's own RLS policies, so serving as the owner would silently switch OFF
# every tenant protection while every test still passed. The app reads ONLY this
# variable (no fallback to DATABASE_URL) and REFUSES to serve if the connected
# role is a superuser or has `rolbypassrls` — see src/db/app-pool.ts. Never put
# the owner url here.
APP_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://authenticated@127.0.0.1:55499/warehouse"

# TEST (non-superuser `authenticated`): every test connects as this role.
# It does not own the tables, so RLS applies to it and a tenancy test proves
# something; run as the owner it would pass trivially. Never put a superuser
# (or `postgres`) here. A test that points the migration runner at this url is
# exactly the bug the ownership regression test guards against.
SQL_TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://authenticated@127.0.0.1:55499/warehouse"

# --- Auth ------------------------------------------------------------------
# SIGN-IN IS BY AN ASSIGNED USERNAME, not a name: `app_users.username` is unique
# per tenant (see `supabase/migrations/0012_usernames.sql`).
#
# The per-account lockout, as deployment configuration. Both are read once at
# boot and VALIDATED: anything that is not a positive integer falls back to the
# default, so a typo can never silently disable the lockout.
#   LOCKOUT_THRESHOLD  — how many failures lock one username out (default 5)
#   LOCKOUT_WINDOW_MS  — the window they are counted over (default 900000 = 15m)
# LOCKOUT_THRESHOLD="5"
# LOCKOUT_WINDOW_MS="900000"
#
# WHICH TENANT THIS DEPLOYMENT SERVES. v1 is one deployment per client, so the
# tenant is deployment CONFIGURATION: the app reads it at boot (src/auth/tenant.ts
# ::configuredTenantId) and pins `app.tenant_id` with it before any session
# exists. Required by Task 11's auth shell; there is deliberately no host/slug
# lookup in the database (see README, "Which tenant this deployment serves").
# Set it to the tenant uuid — a missing or malformed value fails loudly at boot,
# and so does the NIL uuid (00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000), which is a
# placeholder rather than a tenant (see src/auth/tenant.ts::configuredTenantId).
TENANT_ID="11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555"
